<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Homework 03</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Complete the exercises  and aim to submit your work by the due date. As for the MATLAB Lab, create an .m file titled Lab3_YourLastName.m and find eigenvalues and eigenvectors, and check that your diagonalization is correct by multiplying <m>PDP^{-1}</m> for your homework.</p><p>
      This is a guided exercise to make sure you can apply the concepts that we have learned in a new setting. You may use MATLAB to double-check your calculations, but you must show your work.
    </p>
  </introduction>

   <p> <alert>Guided Practice: Eigenvalues, Eigenvectors, and Diagonalization</alert></p>

<ol>
        <li>
            <p>Find the characteristic polynomial <m>\small\det(A-\lambda I)</m> and set it equal 0.</p></li><li>
                <p>
                 The solutions of the previous step are the eigenvalues. They can be repeated, they can be real as well as complex numbers. Find all of them.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Find eigenvectors corresponding to each distinct eigenvalue (if you have repeated ones, perform this step only once) by solving <m>\small(A-\lambda I)x=0</m> and then pick a non-zero solution as <m>\mathbf{v}_i</m> for each eigenvalue.
                </p>
                <p>
                    At this step you will get infinitely many solutions with one or more free variables. Here is how you can pick non-zero solutions: set one of the free variables to 1 and the rest to 0. This will give you a non-zero solution. Then repeat it with the rest of the free variables one by one, e.g.:
                   <me>\small
                    \begin{cases}
                    x=2y+3z\\
                    y\text{ is free}\\
                    z\text{ is free}
                    \end{cases}\Longrightarrow
                    \begin{bmatrix}
                    2y+3z\\
                    y\\
                    z
                    \end{bmatrix}\text{ is a solution},\quad
                    v_1=\begin{bmatrix}
                    2\\
                    1\\
                    0
                    \end{bmatrix}(y=1,z=0),\quad
                    v_2=\begin{bmatrix}
                    3\\
                    0\\
                    1
                    \end{bmatrix}(y=0,z=1)
                   </me>
            
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
            
                    If we get enough eigenvectors this way, we can construct the matrix <m>P</m> by putting the eigenvectors as columns and <m>D</m>, a diagonal matrix with the eigenvalues on the diagonal in the order that corresponds to the eigenvectors in <m>P</m>. Make sure that <m>P</m> is invertible, otherwise the matrix is not diagonalizable.
                    For example if <m>v_1</m> and <m>v_2</m> are eigenvectors corresponding to <m>\lambda_1</m> and <m>\lambda_2</m> respectively, then
                    <me>
                       P=\begin{bmatrix}
                       v_1 &amp; v_2
                       \end{bmatrix},\quad\text{and}\quad
                       D=\small\begin{bmatrix}
                       \lambda_1 &amp; 0\\
                       0 &amp; \lambda_2
                       \end{bmatrix}.
                    </me>
                </p>
            </li>
    </ol>


    <exercise xml:id="practice-2x2">
        <title>Practice Problem 1 (2×2 Matrix)</title>

        <p>
            Consider the matrix
            <me>
            B=\begin{bmatrix}
            3 &amp; 0\\
            4 &amp; -1
            \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
        </p>
<ol>
        <li>
            <p>Find the characteristic polynomial <m>\det(B-\lambda I)</m> and set it equal 0.</p>
                <p>
                    Work:
                </p>
                <p>___<m>\det(B-\lambda I)=</m>_______________</p>
                <p>________________________________________</p>
                <p>________________________________________</p>
                <p>________________________________________</p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>B</m>.</p>
            
                <p>
                    <m>\lambda_1=</m> ____________
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>\lambda_2=</m> ____________
                </p>
            
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>Find an eigenvector corresponding to <m>\lambda_1</m> by solving <m>B-\lambda_1 I=0</m> and then pick a non-zero solution as <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>.</p>
            
                <p>Work:</p>
                <p>________________________________________</p>
                <p>________________________________________</p>
                <p>
                    <m>\mathbf{v}_1=</m> ____________________
                </p>
            
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>Find an eigenvector corresponding to <m>\lambda_2</m> by solving <m>B-\lambda_2 I=0</m> and then pick a non-zero solution as <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m>.</p>
            
                <p>Work:</p>
                <p>________________________________________</p>
                <p>________________________________________</p>
                <p>
                    <m>\mathbf{v}_2=</m> ____________________
                </p>
            
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>Construct the matrices <m>P</m> by putting the eigenvectors as columns and <m>D</m>, a diagonal matrix with the eigenvalues on the diagonal in the order that corresponds to the eigenvectors in <m>P</m>. Make sure that <m>P</m> is invertible.</p>
            
                <p>
                    <m>P=</m> ____________________
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>D=</m> ____________________
                </p>
            
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>Write the diagonalization <m>B=PDP^{-1}</m> and use MATLAB to verify it.</p>
            
                <p>________________________________________</p>
            
        </li>
        </ol>
    </exercise>



